  "notes": [
    "the app isn't named in the file. No source describes its format, so every unit is decided from the file's own values or kept as written",
    "`hPa` read in hectopascals, from its name and the pad: the first reading, on the pad, is 1001.29, within the 500 to 1100 hPa of a launch site",
    "`AltitudeRelativeBarometer` read in meters, from the pressure and the coast: (1) above the first row it lies at most 0.00 from the pressure altitude of `hPa` in the U.S. Standard Atmosphere, in meters. The climb is 1176.8 m, where 26.5 is allowed. (2) in the coast from 8.00 s to 13.00 s it slows by 9.81 per second per second, 1.00 g in meters. Gravity and drag give at least 1 g, and 6.86 to 22.52 m/s² reads meters",
    "`VelocityBarometer` read in meters per second, from the barometric altitude and the coast: (1) against the barometric altitude's rate it has a gain of 1.002, where 0.8 to 1.25 reads m/s. (2) in the coast from 8.00 s to 13.00 s it slows by 9.81 per second per second, 1.00 g in meters. Gravity and drag give at least 1 g, and 6.86 to 22.52 m/s² reads meters",
    "`VelocityBarometerRaw` read in meters per second, from the barometric altitude and the coast: (1) against the barometric altitude's rate it has a gain of 1.002, where 0.8 to 1.25 reads m/s. (2) in the coast from 8.00 s to 13.00 s it slows by 9.79 per second per second, 1.00 g in meters. Gravity and drag give at least 1 g, and 6.86 to 22.52 m/s² reads meters",
    "`Gx`, `Gy`, `Gz` and `GTotal` read in standard gravity, from the pad and the boost: (1) the quietest second before the steepest climb, to -0.40 s, reads 0.990 in the axes' magnitude, where 0.9 to 1.1 reads g at rest. (2) from then to the steepest climb, at 1.50 s, the magnitude less 1 g, read in g, gives 143.2 m/s. That is 1.01 times the barometric climb rate, where 0.5 to 2 reads g",
    "`NoGravGx`, `NoGravGy`, `NoGravGz` and `NoGravGTotal` read in standard gravity, with the accelerometer's axes. The axes less them, the gravity the phone took out, have a median magnitude of 1.000. A median of 0.95 to 1.05 is 1 g",
    "`Yaw` read in radians, from its range and the magnetic field's direction: (1) its largest reading from 0 is 3.1064, where radians stay within ±π. (2) on 28 rows where the accelerometer reads its rest, its y axis is at least 0.2 from vertical and the yaw at least 0.2 from 0, the yaw differs from the azimuth of the phone's y axis from magnetic north, which the magnetometer's and the accelerometer's directions give, by a median 0.000 read in radians and 1.412 in degrees",
    "`Pitch` read in radians, from its range and gravity's direction: (1) its largest reading from 0 is 1.2000, where radians stay within ±π/2. (2) on 31 rows where the accelerometer reads its rest and the pitch is at least 0.2 from level, |sin pitch| differs from gravity's share along y by a median 0.000 read in radians and 0.911 in degrees",
    "`Roll` read in radians, from its range and gravity's direction: (1) its largest reading from 0 is 3.1200, where radians stay within ±π. (2) on 28 rows where the accelerometer reads its rest, its y axis is at least 0.2 from vertical and the roll at least 0.2 from a half-turn, the roll's angle from the nearest half-turn differs from gravity's about y, atan(|Gx| / |Gz|), by a median 0.000 read in radians and 0.978 in degrees",
    "`GyroX`, `GyroY` and `GyroZ` read in radians per second, from the attitude and the full scale: (1) over 21 spans of 5 rows, the attitude turns a median 0.999 times the rate summed, read in radians, where 0.7 to 1.3 reads rad/s. (2) `GyroY` stops at 17.4533 on at least 5 rows, 1000.0 °/s read in rad/s, a gyroscope's full scale of 1000 °/s",
    "`Heading` read in degrees, from its range and its wrap's period: (1) its readings, 0.024 to 357.549, lie within 0 to 360, the largest past the 2π radians reach. (2) 2 steps between rows cross north as a heading in degrees does: by more than 300. Each, less 360, turns as the steps on either side do, within a factor of 2",
    "`Latitude` and `Longitude` read in degrees, from the latitude's range and the longitude's: (1) the latitude's largest reading from 0 is 40.1, where radians reach 1.5708 and degrees 90. (2) the longitude's largest reading from 0 is 100.2, where radians reach 3.1416 and degrees 180",
    "`Timestamp` read in milliseconds since 1970 UTC, from the clock and the date: (1) over 110 steps between rows, its step over 1,000 lies at most 0.0000 s from the clock's, `Seconds`. (2) its first reading, read in ms since 1970, falls between 2000 and 2100; kept as the time of day in UTC",
    "`AltitudeMSLGPS` and `AltitudeMSLMaxGPS` kept as written, in a unit no second check settles: at its 23 fresh fixes it rises 1.000 times as fast as the pressure altitude, near 1 in meters and 3.28 in feet. But that is one check. A GPS's fixes once a second can't show gravity's pull in the coast. A pad's height above sea level can't tell meters from feet within the weather's reach",
    "`Record`, `Static`, `UsbState` and `Satellites` kept as written, as numbers with no unit: by their names flags, states or counts",
    "`GMax`, `AltitudeRelativeMaxBarometer`, `NoGravGMax`, `VelocityBarometerMax` and `VelocityBarometerMin` kept as written, as running extremes. By their names they are the most or least of another column so far. The reader doesn't tie them to that column's unit",
    "`Temperature` kept as written, in a unit no two checks settle. Its readings, 31.5 to 31.5, would be a phone's warmth in degrees Celsius or a cool day's in degrees Fahrenheit. Nothing else in the file tells them apart",
    "`MagnetometerX`, `MagnetometerY`, `MagnetometerZ`, `HeadingX`, `HeadingY` and `HeadingZ` kept as written: by name a magnetometer's reading, in a unit no source states, and the record has no quantity for a magnetic field",
    "`Revolutions`, `RevApogee` and `RevolutionsClock` kept as written: by name a count of turns or its clock, which no check in the file ties to the gyroscope",
    "`HorizontalResGPS` and `VerticalResGPS` kept as written: by name the GPS's accuracy, in a unit no source states",
    "`Battery` kept as written: by name the battery's state, in a unit no source states",
    "`Reserved1`, `Reserved2`, `Reserved3` and `Reserved4` kept as written: reserved, by name; no source states what it holds",
    "`UsbV1`, `UsbV2`, `UsbV3`, `UsbV4`, `UsbT1`, `UsbT2`, `UsbT3`, `UsbT4`, `UsbT5`, `UsbT6`, `UsbUR1`, `UsbUR2`, `UsbUR3`, `UsbUR4`, `UsbUR5`, `UsbUR6`, `UsbUR7` and `UsbUR8` kept as written: by name an external sensor's, in a unit no source states"
  ]
}
